<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Adi Shankara</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 8th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Adi Shankaracharya, Ādi Śaṅkara</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Adi Shankara, also called Adi Shankaracharya, was an Indian Vedic scholar-monk, philosopher and teacher of Advaita <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedanta">Vedanta</a> who lived in the 8th century CE. He wrote commentaries on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahma">Brahma</a> sutras, ten principal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upanishads">Upanishads</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita">Bhagavad Gita</a>, centred on the liberating knowledge that the individual self is Ātman-Brahman. Reliable information on his life is scant: the oldest hagiographies place his birth at Kaladi in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>, and the Shankara of legend who toured the subcontinent defeating opponents in debate and founding four mathas was created in the 14th century. Over 300 texts are attributed to him, most likely by others, and the title Shankaracharya derives from his name.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adi-shankara.jpg" alt="Adi Shankara" width="330" height="466" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Raja Ravi Varma · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Raja_Ravi_Varma_-_Sankaracharya.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ramanuja, traditionally dated 1017–1137, taught at Srirangam that souls and the world are real and form the body of God, a system known as Vishishtadvaita, qualified non-dualism, which he set against the strict non-dualism of <b>Adi Shankara</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/bhakti-movement">Bhakti movement · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Adi Shankara</b>'s commentary (c. 800 CE), the oldest surviving and most influential, reads it as teaching the non-dualism of Atman and Brahman and treats bhakti as a practice that purifies the mind.</blockquote><a href="/work/bhagavad-gita">Bhagavad Gita · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;Bhaja Govindam&quot; (&quot;praise Govinda&quot;, or &quot;seek Govinda&quot;), also called &quot;Moha Mudgara&quot; (&quot;destroyer of illusion&quot;), is a popular Hindu devotional poem in Sanskrit traditionally attributed to <b>Adi Shankara</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/bhaja-govindam">Bhaja Govindam</a></li><li><blockquote>Its authorship is attributed to the theologian <b>Adi Shankara</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/mahishasura-mardini-stotra">Mahishasura Mardini Stotra</a></li></ul></section>
